pseudo-Boolean proof version 2.0
* Regression test (wrong verdict): a top-level rule executed while an order
* definition had its own constraint database installed.
*
* `pre_order`/`def_order` and `spec` return `true` from
* `Checkable::swaps_database`, so from here to the matching `end` the checker
* runs against a fresh, EMPTY sub-database and the formula's is parked in the
* `OrderContext`. The only positional gate `execute_rule` had refused a rule
* that is not `is_subproof_friendly`, and only when the innermost subcontext
* answered `Subcontext::is_subproof()` -- false for exactly the four
* subcontexts an order definition opens. The version 3.0 parser covered the
* gap with its `Scope` ladder; the version 2.0 parser dispatches every line
* through one flat match with no scope state at all, so any rule could be
* written here.
*
* `sol` validates its assignment with `utils::check_solution`, which iterates
* the INSTALLED database -- empty -- so it passed vacuously and set
* `best_valid_objective_value`, which is the whole of the evidence a
* `conclusion SAT` without a hint rests on. The formula is unsatisfiable by
* inspection: `x1 = 1` falsifies `1 ~x1 >= 1` and `x1 = 0` falsifies
* `1 x1 >= 1`. This used to print `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` at exit 0 under
* default flags, from the same binary that verifies its true UNSAT proof in
* correct/version2/order_definition_sol_outside.
f 2
pre_order simple
    sol x1
    vars
        left u1
        right v1
        aux
    end
    def
        -1 u1 1 v1 >= 0 ;
    end
    transitivity
        vars
            fresh_right w1
        end
        proof
            proofgoal #1
                p -2 -3 + -1 +
            end -1
        end
    end
end
output NONE
conclusion SAT
end pseudo-Boolean proof
